text:
maria: quick question — we have a tenant that hit the dedicated pool, then later a private VPC endpoint, and the gateway still routed to the public pool. looks like header hint was ignored.
kevin: can you paste the request headers + resolved pool id? might be a precedence bug.
maria: pasted obfuscated headers:\n```\nX-Workspace: ws_987\nX-RWD-Scope: private_vpc:vpce-1a2b\nHost: api.redwood.dev\nAuthorization: Bearer xxxxx\n```\nresolved pool: pool-public-03
sam: hmm that implies header-hint was dropped before routing decision. do we still prefer workspace-scoped pools over explicit vpc hints? i thought vpce binding should be highest precedence.
olivia: current doc says precedence: VPC binding (explicit tenant->vpc), header scope hint, workspace default pool. but implementation might be flipped in the gateway when Host header is present.
maria: yep — repro steps: curl with Host=api.redwood.dev + X-RWD-Scope set -> sometimes routes to public pool if Host resolves to shared gateway edge.
kevin: that matches an old short-circuit: we validate host against allowed workspace hosts, and if match fails we fall back to host-level routing which ignores X-RWD-Scope. bug.
jordan: risky: if header hints are ignored for host-mismatch, customers with private endpoints could get routed to public GPUs -> data isolation risk. we should treat that as P0 for dedicated/vpc customers.
maria: agreed. what are quick mitigations? two options: (1) ensure header hint is honored regardless of Host check, (2) fail closed when a vpc hint is present but cannot be resolved.
sam: prefer fail closed for now. If request says private_vpc and we can't verify binding, respond 403 or 409 instead of routing to public.
olivia: +1 for fail closed. Also telemetry: we need an event when a header hint is present and routing falls back, include stack id, resolved pool candidate, and decision reason.
kevin: I'll add a concise telemetry schema: event_type=route_resolution, fields=[request_id, workspace, hint, host, resolved_pool, final_pool, decision_reason].
maria: can we also get a console UX flag? the Routes dashboard should show "fallbacked from vpc hint" as a tag so support can quickly triage.
jordan: rollout plan: deploy fail-closed behind a feature flag, gradual 10/50/100 split to tenants with active private endpoints, monitor telemetry for false positives.
sam: tests: unit for precedence order, e2e sim that sends header hint + mismatched Host, assert 403 when flag on.
kevin: i'll attach a minimal patch in a PR later. snippet idea:

```
// routing decision pseudo
if (hint.type == "vpc") {
  if (!validate_vpc_binding(hint, workspace)) {
    if (feature.fail_closed_on_unverified_vpc) {
      return respond(403, "unverified vpc hint");
    }
    log("vpc_hint_unverified", ...);
  } else {
    return route_to(vpc_pool);
  }
}
// continue normal routing
```

olivia: also add an internal alert for any routing that would have gone to public but had a vpc hint — high severity.
maria: +1 — customer impact worst-case is data crossing isolation boundary.
jordan: telemetry thresholds for escalation: >5 incidents in 10m OR any single incident for a paid dedicated customer -> paged.
deploy-bot: deploy queued: feature-flag:fail-closed-on-unverified-vpc -> canary group (eng-staging) scheduled in 10m :rocket:
kevin: I'll open the PR with changelog and test plan. tagging @security for review.
maria: thanks all. i'll assemble a short incident playbook in #incidents if we see any noisy false positives during rollout. :thumbsup:
